## TL;DR  

1. **Upgrade to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0** (the version that understands an array of sprite sheets).  
2. **Turn the `sprite` property into an array** and give your own sheet a unique `id` (e.g. `"my-poi"`).  
3. **Reference the icons with the `id:` prefix** (`"my-poi:coffee"`).  
4. If the style is already loaded, **add the sheet at runtime** with `map.addSprite('my-poi', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi')`.  
5. Verify with `map.hasImage('my-poi:coffee')` / `map.listImages()`.  

That’s all you need – no merging, no rebuilding, no extra CORS tricks (aside from the usual cross-origin headers).

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a few “gotchas” and debugging tips.

---

## 1. Why the icons never appear

When a style has a **single** `sprite` URL, MapLibre only looks in that one sprite sheet.  
If you later add a second sheet somewhere else (e.g. by calling `addImage` for each icon) you would have to load each image individually, which defeats the purpose of a sprite.

Since **MapLibre GL JS 3.0** the `sprite` property can be an **array of `{id, url}` objects**.  
If you keep the old string form, the engine never even knows about your second sheet, so `icon-image: "coffee"` is resolved against the *default* sheet only – it never finds `"coffee"` and silently falls back to “no image”. The console stays quiet because the style *did* load a sprite (the basemap one) successfully; the missing image is just not in that sheet.

---

## 2. The array-form of `sprite`

### 2.1. The JSON you ship with the style

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    {
      "id": "default",                     // <-- the basemap’s sheet
      "url": "https://tiles.provider.org/sprites/basemap"
    },
    {
      "id": "my-poi",                      // <-- your own sheet
      "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi"
    }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "poi",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "places",
      "source-layer": "poi",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "my-poi:coffee",    // <-- note the prefix
        "icon-size": 1.2,
        "icon-allow-overlap": true
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Key points**

| What | Why it matters |
|------|-----------------|
| `id: "default"` | The *default* sheet is the one that can be referenced **without** a prefix. Keep the basemap’s sheet under this id so the original style’s `icon-image` values keep working unchanged. |
| `id: "my-poi"` | Any other id **must** be used as a prefix when you refer to an image from that sheet (`"my-poi:coffee"`). |
| `url` **must not** contain an extension. | MapLibre automatically appends `.json`, `.png`, and `@2x` variants. |
| All `id`s **must be unique**. | Duplicate ids cause a validation error (`all the sprites' ids must be unique`). |
| The array can be mixed with a single string (the string becomes the `default` entry). | If you only need to add one extra sheet you can keep the original string and just add the second entry via `map.addSprite()` (see §4). |

### 2.2. What the sprite files look like

For each entry you need a **pair of files** (plus optional `@2x` versions) on the same origin:

```
/sprites/poi.json
/sprites/poi.png
/sprites/poi@2x.json   (optional)
/sprites/poi@2x.png    (optional)
```

The JSON is a map of image IDs to metadata, e.g.:

```json
{
  "coffee": {
    "width": 24,
    "height": 24,
    "x": 0,
    "y": 0,
    "pixelRatio": 1,
    "sdf": false
  },
  "restaurant": { … }
}
```

> **Tip:** If you generate the sprite with `spritezero`, `spreet`, or Martin, they will create the correct JSON for you automatically.

---

## 3. Using the new sheet in your layers

### 3.1. Symbol-layer `icon-image`

```js
layout: {
  "icon-image": "my-poi:coffee",   // <-- prefix with the sheet id
  "icon-size": 1,
  "icon-allow-overlap": true
}
```

If you forget the prefix, MapLibre will look only in the `default` sheet and silently skip the icon.

### 3.2. Expressions

If you build the name dynamically (e.g. from a feature property), you still need the prefix:

```js
'icon-image': [
  'concat',
  'my-poi:',
  ['get', 'icon_name']   // property value like "coffee"
]
```

---

## 4. Adding a sheet *after* the style has loaded (runtime)

Sometimes you cannot edit the style JSON (e.g. the style comes from a third-party service). In that case you can inject your sheet **once the map has emitted `load`**:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the extra sprite sheet
  map.addSprite('my-poi', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi');

  // 2️⃣ (Optional) Verify it arrived
  map.once('styledata', () => {
    console.log('All sprite sheets loaded');
    console.log('Has coffee?', map.hasImage('my-poi:coffee'));
  });

  // 3️⃣ Add / update your POI layer (or any layer that uses the new icons)
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-poi-layer',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'my-poi-source',
    layout: {
      'icon-image': 'my-poi:coffee',
      'icon-size': 1
    }
  });
});
```

**Why `addSprite` works:**  
When the style’s `sprite` property is a *string*, MapLibre internally treats it as the `default` entry. `addSprite(id, url)` simply appends a new entry to the internal array, so you get the same effect as editing the JSON.

> **Note:** `addSprite` must be called **after** the style’s `load` event, otherwise the map will try to fetch the sheet before the style is ready and will ignore it.

---

## 5. Debugging checklist

| Symptom | Check |
|---------|-------|
| **My POI icons never appear, no console error** | 1. Open the Network tab → look for `.../sprites/poi.json` and `.../sprites/poi.png`.  <br>2. Verify they return **200** and have the correct **CORS** header (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain). |
| **`map.hasImage('my-poi:coffee')` returns `false`** | • The sheet either didn’t load or the JSON does not contain the key. <br>• Open the JSON file directly in the browser and search for `"coffee"`. |
| **`map.hasImage('coffee')` returns `true` but the icon is still missing** | • You are referencing the *default* sheet (no prefix). <br>• Ensure the icon name in the sprite JSON matches exactly (case-sensitive). |
| **`styleimagemissing` fires with `"my-poi:coffee"`** | • The sheet loaded, but the ID is wrong (typo, missing prefix, or the JSON key is different). |
| **CORS error** | • The sprite PNG/JSON must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` that includes your page’s origin (or `*`). <br>• If you’re using a CDN, enable CORS on the bucket. |
| **Using an old MapLibre version (< 3.0)** | • The `sprite` array is ignored → you’ll see the same “nothing shows” behavior. <br>• Upgrade to the latest stable version (`npm i maplibre-gl@latest`). |

---

## 6. Full example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – multiple sprite sheets</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // 1️⃣ Keep the basemap’s sprite as the default entry
      sprite: [
        { id: 'default', url: 'https://tiles.provider.org/sprites/basemap' },
        { id: 'my-poi',  url: 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi' }
      ],
      sources: {
        osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' },
        // 2️⃣ POI layer that uses the extra sheet
        {
          id: 'my-poi',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: {
            type: 'geojson',
            data: {
              type: 'FeatureCollection',
              features: [
                { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] }, properties: { name: 'Coffee' } }
              ]
            }
          },
          layout: {
            'icon-image': 'my-poi:coffee',
            'icon-size': 1.2,
            'icon-allow-overlap': true
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 13
  });

  // Optional: confirm the sheet loaded
  map.on('load', () => {
    console.log('Has coffee?', map.hasImage('my-poi:coffee')); // true → good
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*If you cannot edit the style JSON* (e.g. you receive it from a third-party endpoint), replace the `style` object above with the URL to the remote style, and after `load` call:

```js
map.addSprite('my-poi', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi');
```

Then update your layer’s `icon-image` to use the prefix.

---

## 7. When you *must* stay on an older MapLibre version

If you are stuck on a version **prior to 3.0** (e.g. a legacy app that cannot be upgraded), the only options are:

| Option | What you do |
|--------|-------------|
| **Merge the two sheets** (using `spritezero` or similar) | Create a single PNG+JSON that contains both the basemap icons and your POI icons. |
| **Load your POI icons with `addImage`** (one request per icon) | Use `map.loadImage` → `map.addImage` for each POI icon. This works but loses the batching advantage of a sprite. |
| **Upgrade** | The array-form is the cleanest solution; consider a small upgrade to the latest 3.x or 4.x release (no breaking API changes for this feature). |

---

## 8. Quick reference cheat-sheet

| Action | Code snippet |
|--------|--------------|
| **Declare two sheets in the style JSON** | `"sprite": [{ "id

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6** (the current stable version at the time of writing).

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain, rasterised into an `HTMLImageElement`, and registered with `map.addImage()`.  
* A GeoJSON source with ~5 000 points is added.  
* The symbol layer uses the feature property `category` to pick the right icon (`icon-image: ['get','category']`).  
* Collision handling is left at the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) so icons that would overlap are automatically hidden.  
* A small extra trick (`icon-optional: true`) makes the **text label** (if you ever add one) appear even when the icon is culled, but the icons themselves still respect collision detection.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 (replace with the exact version you use) -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Map initialisation
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],   // San Francisco (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  List of the 40+ categories you have SVGs for
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // Example – replace with your real list.
  const categories = [
    'cafe', 'restaurant', 'park', 'museum', 'library',
    'hospital', 'school', 'bank', 'pharmacy', 'hotel',
    // … up to 40+ …
  ];

  // Base URL where the SVGs live (no trailing slash)
  const SVG_BASE = 'https://my-domain.example.com/icons';

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG and rasterise it into an Image
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  function loadSvgAsImage(name) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const img = new Image(24, 24);               // size you want on the map
      img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';               // required for CORS-enabled SVGs
      img.onload  = () => resolve({ name, img });
      img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`SVG "${name}" failed to load`));
      img.src = `${SVG_BASE}/${name}.svg`;
    });
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Load *all* SVGs in parallel, then register them with the map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  async function registerAllIcons() {
    const promises = categories.map(loadSvgAsImage);
    const results   = await Promise.all(promises);   // throws if any SVG fails

    for (const {name, img} of results) {
      // Guard against a name that already exists in the style (unlikely here)
      if (!map.hasImage(name)) {
        // `sdf: false` – we are not using signed-distance-field icons
        map.addImage(name, img, { sdf: false });
      }
    }
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  After the style is ready, register icons, add source + layer
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', async () => {
    try {
      await registerAllIcons();                     // 5️⃣
    } catch (e) {
      console.error(e);
      // If you want the map to continue even with a missing icon,
      // you could fall back to a generic placeholder here.
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    // 7️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 venues)
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('venues', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://my-domain.example.com/venues.geojson'   // <-- your file
    });

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    // 8️⃣  Symbol layer that uses the icons
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'venue-icons',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'venues',
      // The icon name is taken directly from the feature property `category`
      layout: {
        'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],   // e.g. "cafe", "park", …
        'icon-size': 1,                      // adjust if you need it larger/smaller
        // Collision handling – default is false, we keep it explicit:
        'icon-allow-overlap': false,
        // Optional: let a text label appear even when the icon is culled.
        // (Only useful if you later add a `text-field`.)
        'icon-optional': true
      },
      paint: {
        // If you ever switch to SDF icons you could colour them here.
        // 'icon-color': '#ff6600'
      }
    });

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    // 9️⃣  (Optional) Listen for missing-image warnings – helpful in dev
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    map.on('styleimagemissing', e => {
      console.warn(`⚠️  Image "${e.id}" is not in any sprite sheet nor added via addImage`);
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## How the pieces work together

| Step | What happens | Why it matters for your use-case |
|------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| **2️⃣ Map init** | Loads a base style (any style that already contains a `sprite` URL). | The style provides the background, roads, etc. – you only add your own icons. |
| **3️⃣ Category list** | You enumerate every possible `category` value that appears in the GeoJSON. | `addImage` must be called **once per distinct icon name** before the layer can reference it. |
| **4️⃣ `loadSvgAsImage`** | Creates an `Image` element, sets `crossOrigin='anonymous'`, and waits for `onload`. | SVGs are rasterised on the client; the resulting bitmap is what MapLibre can draw. |
| **5️⃣ `registerAllIcons`** | Calls `map.addImage(name, img, {sdf:false})` for each SVG. | After registration the name (`cafe`, `park`, …) becomes a valid `icon-image` value. |
| **6️⃣ `map.on('load')`** | Guarantees the style is fully parsed before we touch it. | `addImage` must be called **after** the style has loaded, otherwise the map will reject the call. |
| **7️⃣ GeoJSON source** | Loads the 5 000 venue points in one request. | A single source is far more efficient than 5 000 separate `Marker`s. |
| **8️⃣ Symbol layer** | `icon-image: ['get','category']` picks the right icon per feature. | No per-feature code is needed – the style does the lookup for you. |
| **Collision handling** | `icon-allow-overlap: false` (default) hides icons that would overlap. | When you zoom out the map stays readable; only the most “important” icons survive (based on `symbol-sort-key` and the internal placement algorithm). |
| **9️⃣ `styleimagemissing`** | Fires once per missing ID, logged to the console. | Great for debugging – you’ll instantly see a typo or a forgotten SVG. |

---

## Tweaking the appearance

| Need | Change |
|------|--------|
| **Larger icons** | Adjust `'icon-size': 1.5` (or any number). |
| **Different anchor** | Add `'icon-anchor': 'bottom'` (or `center`, `top`, …). |
| **Text label underneath** | Add a `text-field` layout property, e.g. `'text-field': ['get','name']`. |
| **Stretchable badges (e.g., route shields)** | When you build a sprite, set `stretchX`/`stretchY` and then add `'icon-text-fit': 'both'` to the layer. |
| **SDF icons (single-color, recolourable)** | Build the sprite with SDF images and register with `{sdf:true}`; then you can use `'icon-color'` and `'icon-halo-color'`. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how we avoided them

| Pitfall | What we did |
|---------|-------------|
| **Using `map.loadImage()` for SVG** | `loadImage` only supports raster formats (PNG, JPEG, WebP). We rasterise the SVG ourselves via an `Image` element. |
| **CORS errors** | `img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous'` and the SVG files must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Forgot to wait for the `load` event** | All `addImage` calls happen inside `map.on('load', …)`. |
| **Duplicate IDs** | `if (!map.hasImage(name))` guards against accidental re-registration. |
| **Missing icons silently failing** | The `styleimagemissing` listener prints a clear warning. |
| **Icons piling up** | Default collision detection (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) already hides overlapping icons; you can further tune with `symbol-sort-key` if you need a custom priority. |

---

### 🎉 That's it!

*Copy the HTML above, replace the placeholder URLs (`SVG_BASE`, the GeoJSON URL, and optionally the base style), and you’ll have a performant map that shows 5 000 venues with their category-specific SVG icons, automatically de-cluttering when you zoom out.*

Below is a **complete, minimal example** that

1. **Creates a draggable pin (a `Marker`).**  
2. **Positions it at a given longitude-latitude.**  
3. **Shows a `Popup` when the user clicks the pin** (the popup also follows the pin while it is being dragged).

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin with popup</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],             // initial view (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Wait until the style is ready – then add the marker
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create a Popup (but don’t add it yet)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
      offset: 25,               // distance from the pin
      closeButton: true,
      closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
    }).setHTML(`
      <h4>San Francisco</h4>
      <p>Drag the pin to a new location.</p>
    `);

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Create a draggable Marker
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
      draggable: true,          // <-- makes it draggable
      // You can also pass a custom HTML element here:
      // element: document.createElement('div')
    })
      .setLngLat([-122.42, 37.77])   // initial coordinate
      .setPopup(popup)               // associate the popup with the marker
      .addTo(map);                   // finally add it to the map

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ Open the popup when the user clicks the pin
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The Marker already forwards click events to its popup,
    // so the simplest way is just to call `togglePopup()` on click.
    // (If you want more control you can listen to the DOM event.)
    marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => {
      // `togglePopup` opens it if closed, closes it if open.
      // `togglePopup()` is a convenience wrapper around `popup.toggle()`.
      marker.togglePopup();
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣ (Optional) React to the drag end – e.g. show new coords
    // -------------------------------------------------
    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
      console.log('Marker dropped at', lngLat);
      // Update the popup content with the new position, if you like:
      popup.setHTML(`
        <h4>New position</h4>
        <p>Lon: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>Lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}</p>
      `);
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does (step-by-step)

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` creates the canvas, loads the style, and sets the initial view. | You need a map instance before you can add any UI element. |
| **2️⃣ Wait for `load`** | `map.on('load', …)` guarantees the style (including any sprite sheet) is ready. | Markers are **outside** the style, but it’s good practice to wait for the map to be ready before touching the DOM. |
| **3️⃣ Create a `Popup`** | `new maplibregl.Popup()` builds the HTML bubble that will appear on click. | Popups can be reused; we attach it to the marker later. |
| **4️⃣ Create a draggable `Marker`** | `new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })` makes the pin draggable. `setLngLat` positions it, `setPopup` links the popup, `addTo(map)` puts it on the map. | A `Marker` is an HTML element that sits **above** every layer, so it never collides with other symbols. |
| **5️⃣ Open the popup on click** | Adding a click listener to `marker.getElement()` and calling `marker.togglePopup()` opens/closes the popup. | The marker already knows about its popup, so `togglePopup()` is the simplest API. |
| **6️⃣ React to drag end** (optional) | `marker.on('dragend', …)` lets you read the new coordinates and, for example, update the popup text. | Useful for UI where the user is moving a pin to select a location (e.g., a geocoder, a “pick-a-point” tool). |

### Quick checklist for a production implementation

| ✅ Checklist | Details |
|--------------|---------|
| **Map version** | The example uses MapLibre GL JS 4.x (the current stable at time of writing). Adjust the CDN URL if you pin a different version. |
| **CORS** | The style URL (`style.json`) and any custom sprite or tile URLs must be CORS-enabled for the domain serving your page. |
| **Accessibility** | If you need keyboard support, you can give the marker element a `tabindex="0"` and listen for `keydown` events. |
| **Custom pin graphics** | Pass `element: myDiv` to `new maplibregl.Marker({ element: myDiv })` where `myDiv` contains any SVG/PNG you like. |
| **Mobile drag** | `draggable: true` works on touch devices out of the box. |
| **Performance** | A single marker is cheap. If you need thousands of points, use a **symbol layer** with a sprite instead of many `Marker`s. |

---

#### TL;DR (one-liner)

```js
new maplibregl.Marker({draggable:true})
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])
  .setPopup(new maplibregl.Popup().setHTML('…'))
  .addTo(map)
  .getElement().addEventListener('click', e => e.target.togglePopup());
```

That’s all you need to place a draggable pin and show a popup when the user clicks it in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
